## Summary

Started from the TODO item "When search still embeds on every keystroke" (Helios When). Its note said this was rare in the logs and that debouncing would change when results appear.

**Investigation.** Production logs since 2026-10-19 show one burst from When's search: 25 searches in 10s, 105–185ms each, from `when.heliosian.com/mine`. Nothing on the path serializes the searches. The browser posts at once over HTTP/2. `Searcher.Search` holds the index lock only to snapshot it. `artifacts.Vertex.Embed` is a plain POST with no mutex or rate limiter. Three requests arriving within 1ms of each other each took about 150ms.

**What ian decided.** The number of Vertex calls doesn't matter. What matters is that the newest result wins and nothing older replaces it. Answers should show as they arrive, but never go backward. When several have arrived, show only the newest instead of replaying each one.

**Built** (`web/when/chrome.js`, `web/when/state.js`, `docs/when/calendar.md`):
- Before: an answer showed only if the box still held the text it was asked for, so results waited for a pause in typing as long as one round trip.
  Now: each keystroke's search is numbered. Once per `requestAnimationFrame`, the newest answer that has arrived is shown, unless an answer to later typing already shows. `findSearch` just returns the found set.
- Before: pending answers were dropped whenever the page redrew.
  Now: `clearSearch` drops them only when no text is carried into the new render. Filters, the classroom and category chips, and switching calendars carry the text. A reviewer caught this regression in an earlier snapshot.

Verified with a local chromedp script (`local/searchorder`, gitignored) against the sample server. It slowed answers so they came back out of order and in bunches, and opened Filters mid-search.

**Left out on purpose:** reducing the per-keystroke embedding calls. Options weighed: debouncing, one search in flight at a time, frame batching of requests, a server LRU of embeddings, and aborting superseded fetches. ian said volume doesn't matter, and the reviewer findings asking for it were dismissed on that basis.

