Each option below is worked through on that same burst.

- (*) Leave it and drop the TODO item
  26 Vertex calls, results exactly as today. One burst a week costs about 26 × 150ms of Vertex time; nothing a person waits on gets slower or faster. Simplest outcome: no code, the item comes off TODO.md.
- ( ) Send once per animation frame
  The input listener records the text and asks for one search on the next frame (about 16ms later) with whatever the box holds then. Input events fired together collapse into one: the groups at 06.329, 10.927, 11.812–11.816 and 13.736–13.747 become one search each. **26 → about 19 calls**; each result appears at most one frame later than today. A few lines in `chrome.js`.
- ( ) One search in flight at a time
  While a search is out, new typing waits; when the answer returns, the latest text is sent if it changed. Simulated on the burst: **26 → 23 calls**, and the final result can arrive up to one round trip (about 150ms) later than today — the change in timing the TODO item warns about.
- ( ) Cache embeddings on the server
  An LRU of text → vector in `Searcher`, like `geocode.Suggestions`' cache of addresses. Saves a call only when the exact same text is searched again (backspacing, retyping); each prefix of a word is new text, so on a typed burst like this one the saving is probably near zero (the log doesn't record the words, so I can't count it). Results' timing unchanged; adds a cache to the server, which the server otherwise avoids.

I recommend leaving it: the saving from any change is a handful of 150ms calls a week, and the two options that save the most still change behaviour (frame batching slightly, in-flight limiting noticeably).

