### There is no queue anywhere on When's search path: every keystroke's search starts as soon as it's typed and runs alongside the older ones, and the page shows only the newest answer. Should I make no code change and just take the item off TODO.md?

**What you asked.** Is there any serialization between When's per-keystroke searches, or can a newer one jump whatever queue there is? You don't mind the volume; you want the newest search to come first (LIFO) or older, unstarted ones to be cancelled.

**What I found, hop by hop.** I followed one keystroke's request from the browser to Vertex and back:

- **Browser.** `web/when/chrome.js`: the search box's `input` listener calls `search(typed())`, which calls `findSearch` in `web/when/state.js`. That function posts to `/api/do/search` right away. Nothing waits for an earlier request first: there's no queue and no in-flight check. The browser reaches Google's front end over HTTP/2 (the logged protocol between the front end and Cloud Run is `H2C`), so every request shares one connection, and HTTP/1.1's six-connection limit doesn't apply.
- **Server.** Each request runs in its own goroutine. `Searcher.Search` (`internal/db/search.go`) holds the index's read lock only long enough to take a snapshot (`x.snapshot()`), not across the Vertex call. The background indexer also never holds the lock while it embeds.
- **Vertex.** `artifacts.Vertex.Embed` (`internal/artifacts/embed.go`) is a plain HTTP POST on a shared `http.Client`, with no mutex and no rate limiter.
- **Answers that arrive out of order.** `findSearch` numbers each request (`searchRun`) and stores the hits only if no newer request has started since. `search()` in `chrome.js` then shows results only if the box still holds that text. A late answer to an older keystroke is thrown away; it never replaces a newer one.

**Evidence from production** (2026-10-20 18:27, one person on `when.heliosian.com/mine`): three requests arrived within 1ms of each other at 06.328–06.330. They took 154ms, 176ms and 137ms. Single requests in the same burst took 105–185ms. If the searches were serialized, the third would have taken about 450ms.

<details><summary>That moment in the request log</summary>

```
18:27:06.154001  H2C  0.150826s
18:27:06.328557  H2C  0.154114s
18:27:06.328566  H2C  0.176466s
18:27:06.329539  H2C  0.137349s
18:27:06.924397  H2C  0.158270s
```
</details>

**So:** the newest keystroke never waits behind an older one, and nothing older gets to show over it. Older requests aren't cancelled; they finish and are ignored. Since there's no queue, there's nothing unstarted to cancel. Aborting them with an `AbortController` would save only the volume, which you said you don't care about.

- (*) No code change; remove the item from TODO.md
  The work is just deleting the "When search still embeds on every keystroke" item under Helios When in TODO.md. Search behaves exactly as it does today.
- ( ) Also abort superseded requests
  `findSearch` keeps an `AbortController` and aborts the previous request when a new one starts. The server's request context is then cancelled, which cancels its Vertex call. What people see doesn't change. Each cancelled request shows up as a failed fetch in the browser, and `api()` has to treat an abort as silent rather than as an error. On the 10-20 burst, about 20 of the 26 Vertex calls would be cut off partway, but they would still have started.

