### Should When's search box show each search's answer as it arrives, even when the box has moved on, as long as it is newer than the answer already showing (so results never go backward)?

**How it works today.** Every keystroke in When's search box posts a search right away (`findSearch` in `web/when/state.js`). They all run at once; there's no queue. When an answer comes back, `search()` in `web/when/chrome.js` shows it only if the box still holds the text it was asked for. That means updating the results dropdown and calling the page's handler, which on the home page sets `state.query` and repaints the day, the rail, the filters, Upcoming and the month. Any other answer is thrown away. So results update only when the person pauses longer than one round trip.

**Your proposal, made concrete.** Each search gets a number in the order it was typed. When an answer arrives, it's shown if its number is higher than the answer currently showing, and thrown away otherwise. The dropdown and calendar then show results for the text that answer was asked for (say "fie" while the box already says "fiel"), and catch up as newer answers land. The last keystroke's answer always ends up showing, because nothing older can replace it. The input box itself is never touched.

**Worked on the real burst** (2026-10-20 18:27, one person on `when.heliosian.com/mine`, 25 When searches; I said 26 earlier, which miscounted one of the server's own log lines). For each request I used its arrival time and its measured latency. "Shown" means the answer appeared on screen.

